.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_MODIFY 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_modify \- change an existing fd registration's direction
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_event_loop_modify(ccol_event_loop loop, ccol_event_reg reg,
                        ccol_select_dir new_dir);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function changes the direction of reg in place, with no window
where the fd below it is unregistered. The function works on an fd
only: a call on a registration that comes from a queue selectable or a
ccol_channel selectable returns ccol_invalid_args, because the
direction of a queue selectable is a part of its identity. Remove such
a registration and add it again instead of modifying it.

On success three things happen:
  - The library updates reg->sel.dir, so a callback that the loop
    dispatches after this call returns sees the new direction in the
    sel parameter that it gets. The dispatch path copies reg->sel
    under its own internal lock at the moment that it collects each
    dispatch, and keeps no live pointer into reg->sel, so a callback
    that is already in flight when you call this function keeps the
    direction that was current at the moment of the collect and never
    races this update.
  - reg moves to the other slot on the shared epoll entry of the fd
    below it.
  - The library recomputes the combined epoll interest mask of the fd,
    fresh from the live state of both directions; ordinarily this is a
    single EPOLL_CTL_MOD call. reg itself may be paused at the time of
    the call (see ccol_event_loop_pause(3)). If it is, and the fd has
    no other direction that is not paused, the mask carries no real
    interest: the pause has put the fd outside the epoll interest set
    of the kernel, the fd stays that way, and no epoll_ctl call is
    needed at all. A reg that the loop stopped watching because an
    event reached it that none of its handlers can take (see
    ccol_event_loop_add(3)) is watched again in its new direction.

A call whose new_dir is the current direction of reg changes nothing
else and returns ccol_success, but it makes a reg that the loop
stopped watching, as described above, watched again.

This call is valid only when a different registration on the same fd
does not already hold the slot of the target direction. Use it, for
example, for a non-blocking connect, which starts with an interest in
write and moves to an interest in read after the connect finishes; it
then needs no second ccol_event_loop_add() call.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop     ccol_event_loop the registration belongs to.
reg      Registration to modify.
new_dir  ccol_select_read or ccol_select_write.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success        The function changed the direction.
ccol_invalid_args   loop is CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID, or a handle that
                    is stale or that something already destroyed; or
                    reg is CCOL_EVENT_REG_INVALID, or it fails to
                    resolve for another reason; or reg is a
                    registration for a queue or for a ccol_channel; or
                    new_dir is neither ccol_select_read nor
                    ccol_select_write. A reg that fails to resolve
                    covers a handle that is plainly invalid, and also
                    one that something removed between the moment that
                    the caller got it and this call, either from
                    another thread or from the callback of reg that
                    removes itself. The library finds either case
                    through the registration table of reg and rejects
                    it cleanly; it never causes a use-after-free.
ccol_not_permitted  A different registration on the same fd already
                    holds the target direction.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call at the same time as ccol_event_loop_remove(), and from
inside a callback that runs on one of the reactor threads.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_event_handlers_t handlers = { .on_writable = on_connected, .on_readable = on_data };
ccol_event_reg reg = ccol_event_loop_add(loop, ccol_selectable_from_fd(sock, ccol_select_write),
                               handlers, ctx, NULL);
/* ... inside on_connected, after the non-blocking connect finishes ... */
ccol_event_loop_modify(loop, reg, ccol_select_read);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_event_loop_add (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_remove (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_pause (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_resume (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_reg_generation (3)
